WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.162
INSERT adds a new row to a table In this example we

00:00:04.188 --> 00:00:09.350
are adding one fictional patient The statement
begins with INSERT INTO patients

00:00:10.212 --> 00:00:14.500
Inside parentheses we list the columns that we are
providing MRN full

00:00:14.525 --> 00:00:19.075
name age and gender Naming the columns is a good
habit because

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.225
it makes the statement clearer and less dependent on
the physical order

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:26.612
of columns in the table The VALUES section provides
one value for

00:00:26.638 --> 00:00:32.200
each listed column The order must match the column
list Text values

00:00:32.225 --> 00:00:36.550
are surrounded by single quotes The age is numeric
so it does

00:00:36.562 --> 00:00:41.900
not need quotes After the values we use RETURNING
This is a

00:00:41.925 --> 00:00:47.038
very useful PostgreSQL feature Instead of inserting
the row and then wondering

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.712
what happened RETURNING immediately shows selected
fields from the row that was

00:00:50.738 --> 00:00:55.388
stored It can also show generated values such as a
patient ID

00:00:56.262 --> 00:01:00.638
Run the statement and inspect the returned row Check
that the MRN

00:01:00.875 --> 00:01:05.400
name age and gender match what you intended In a
real workflow

00:01:05.575 --> 00:01:08.612
you may also run a separate SELECT afterward using
the new row's

00:01:08.638 --> 00:01:13.675
stable identifier The main idea is simple add the
row then verify

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.638
it Do not treat an INSERT as complete until you have
confirmed

00:01:17.662 --> 00:01:18.500
what was stored
